# systemd unit whose only job is to hold the shutdown open between "the pods
# are gone" and "the NFS transport is stopped".
#
# See k3s-nfs-drain.sh for the measurements and the reasoning; in short, every
# NFS mount on this node is carried by stunnel, systemd used to stop stunnel
# one second into the shutdown, and roughly sixty containers then spent three
# to four minutes blocked on `hard` NFS mounts with no transport underneath.
#
# Ordering is the whole unit, and systemd stops units in the REVERSE of their
# start order:
#
#   After=stunnel.service              => STOPS BEFORE stunnel
#   After=data-nfs-k3svolumes.mount    => STOPS BEFORE that mount is detached
#   Before=k3s.service                 => STOPS AFTER k3s
#
# giving a stop sequence of:
#
#   k3s -> this unit's ExecStop -> /data/nfs/k3svolumes -> stunnel
#
# Because a unit's stop job does not complete until its ExecStop returns,
# everything below it in that list is held up for exactly as long as the
# drain takes, which is the point: no NFS is torn down anywhere on this node
# until the pods that were using it are gone.
#
# The mount ordering is not redundant with the stunnel ordering. Without it
# the two are unordered siblings, and systemd duly detached
# /data/nfs/k3svolumes at t+0 on 2026-08-11 -- concurrently with the drain,
# while every pod was still running. It survived only because nothing held
# that path at that moment; a pod with a hostPath under it (which is what the
# mount is there for, see k3s-nfs-ordering.conf) would have made the unmount
# busy, and forced or lazy detach puts us straight back into the
# unflushable-dirty-pages state this unit exists to prevent.
#
# Both relationships are ordering-only (no Requires/Wants), matching the style
# of nfs-shutdown-marker.service and the k3s/mount drop-ins alongside it: this
# adds nothing to the boot transaction that could fail it, and a node running
# without stunnel still starts normally.
#
# RemainAfterExit=yes with a no-op ExecStart is the standard idiom for "run
# something specifically at shutdown": ExecStop only fires for a unit that is
# active, and there is nothing to do while the node is running.
#
# TimeoutStopSec must exceed everything the drain can spend: GRACE_TIMEOUT
# (30s) waiting for containers, plus up to 10s for the kill to be reaped,
# plus the unmount sweep. Were systemd to cut the drain short it would stop
# stunnel anyway, which is the exact failure being fixed. 120s is generous
# room for that ~45s worst case and still fits inside the 240s that f3sctl's
# agent gives this guest.
#
# Deploy via Rex: rex -f f3s/r-nodes/Rexfile nfs_mount_monitor

[Unit]
Description=Drain kubelet NFS mounts before the stunnel transport stops
After=stunnel.service
After=data-nfs-k3svolumes.mount
Before=k3s.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/usr/bin/true
ExecStop=/usr/local/bin/k3s-nfs-drain.sh
TimeoutStopSec=120

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
